  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-BGD229</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Men and women in Bangladesh benefit from gender equal, non-discriminative, inclusive and harassment-free care and decent work opportunities for all</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh faces gender inequalities in the world of work in the form of gender-based violence and harassment (GBVH), disproportionate unpaid care responsibilities, underrepresentation in leadership, and limited access to gender-responsive workplaces. These challenges are acute for women with disabilities, indigenous women, and those from marginalized communities. Women remain concentrated in informal, low-paid jobs, facing occupational segregation, wage gaps, and barriers to participation in decision-making processes, due to poor implementation of laws, weak care infrastructure, discriminatory social norms, and incoherence of policies.
ILO will support the Government and partners to improve the legal, institutional, and policy environment for women; and strengthen capacities to implement the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (UNCRPD), develop a structured childcare services framework, improve labour rights protections for childcare workers, and align legislation with ILO Convention 190 to prevent violence and harassment at work. The strategy emphasizes multi-level engagement: policy reforms, quality care services, disability-inclusive employment, and employer capacity to create gender-responsive workplaces.
Expected results include national frameworks and capacities to implement UNCRPD and C190, accessibility and affordability of childcare services, protections for care workers, and adoption of gender-responsive policies. Beneficiaries include women workers, those with disabilities, indigenous women, and marginalized groups. Employers and private sector benefit from improved compliance frameworks and support services.
ILO&#x2019;s partners include key government ministries, including Labour and Employment, Women and Children Affairs, Social Welfare, Industries, and Youth and Sports, along with employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organizations, chambers of commerce, UN agencies, and development partners.</narrative>
  </description>
